  "query_preview": "..\nA trade-wind trough from the east made its approach today, bringing \nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms across the Marianas. The\nheaviest of showers is occurring over the CNMI, significantly \nshearing off near Guam. Shower activity will continue through Sunday,\nbut will downtrend after tonight. As of now, thunderstorm potential \nlooks to be little to none based on drier air aloft continuing to cap\nany vertical growth, but this will be monitored overnight. Based on \nlatest satellite imagery, shower activity may not even last through \nSunday morning, but a surge in moisture upstream of the trough should\nallow showers to linger. Beginning Sunday night, isolated showers \nwill return to the region throughout the rest of the forecast period.\n\nAccording to the U.S. Drought Monitor, Guam is abnormally dry (D0)\nand the CNMI remains unchanged. Hopefully, rainfall tonight can\nbring temporary relief, but drought conditions may continue and \npossibly worsen afterwards as the dry season approaches. Water \nconservation is encouraged over the next several weeks.",
